  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n1 0 0 5 0 1 0\n0 1 0 5 1 1 1\n1 0 0 5 0 0 0\nOutput:\n0 0 0\n0 2 0\n0 0 0\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n1 1 0 5 0 1 0\n0 0 1 5 1 1 1\n1 1 0 5 0 1 0\nOutput:\n0 2 0\n0 0 2\n0 2 0\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n0 0 1 5 0 0 0\n1 1 0 5 1 0 1\n0 1 1 5 1 0 1\nOutput:\n0 0 0\n2 0 0\n0 0 2\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n1 0 1 5 1 0 1\n0 1 0 5 1 0 1\n1 0 1 5 0 1 0\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    \"\"\"\n    Transform a grid that contains a left part, a separator column, and a right part.\n    The separator column is identified by the value '5' (or any constant not 0 or 1).\n    For each cell (i, j) of the left and right parts, the output cell is 2 if both\n    corresponding cells are 1, otherwise 0.\n    The result is the left\u2011right part size (i.e. without the separator column).\n    \"\"\"\n    # Find the separator column (the column containing a value that is not 0 or 1).\n    # In the provided examples this is the value 5.\n    sep_col = None\n    for c, val in enumerate(grid[0]):\n        if val not in (0, 1):\n            sep_col = c\n            break\n    if sep_col is None:\n        # If no obvious separator, assume middle column for odd\u2011width grids.\n        sep_col = len(grid[0]) // 2\n\n    # Split into left and right parts.\n    left = [row[:sep_col] for row in grid]\n    right = [row[sep_col + 1:] for row in grid]\n\n    # Compute the output: 2 where both left and right have 1, else 0.\n    out = []\n    for i in range(len(left)):\n        out_row = []\n        for j in range(len(left[i])):\n            if left[i][j] == 1 and right[i][j] == 1:\n                out_row.append(2)\n            else:\n                out_row.append(0)\n        out.append(out_row)\n\n    return out",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    \"\"\"\n    Transform a grid that contains a left part, a separator column, and a right part.\n    The separator column is identified by the value '5' (or any constant not 0 or 1).\n    For each cell (i, j) of the left and right parts, the output cell is 2 if both\n    corresponding cells are 1, otherwise 0.\n    The result is the left\u2011right part size (i.e. without the separator column).\n    \"\"\"\n    # Find the separator column (the column containing a value that is not 0 or 1).\n    # In the provided examples this is the value 5.\n    sep_col = None\n    for c, val in enumerate(grid[0]):\n        if val not in (0, 1):\n            sep_col = c\n            break\n    if sep_col is None:\n        # If no obvious separator, assume middle column for odd\u2011width grids.\n        sep_col = len(grid[0]) // 2\n\n    # Split into left and right parts.\n    left = [row[:sep_col] for row in grid]\n    right = [row[sep_col + 1:] for row in grid]\n\n    # Compute the output: 2 where both left and right have 1, else 0.\n    out = []\n    for i in range(len(left)):\n        out_row = []\n        for j in range(len(left[i])):\n            if left[i][j] == 1 and right[i][j] == 1:\n                out_row.append(2)\n            else:\n                out_row.append(0)\n        out.append(out_row)\n\n    return out",
